F52 localised (R84a's method): the VACASK corpus deck demo/spice/dio.sim's diode, rs=1.0 is=1e-9 tt=1e-6 cjo=50n
bv=5 ibv=1e-6, ngspice-45 jfngv2gd3zvi5cmr9vrqfhnziipqd09n against VACASK dhxz6l3iac3r2b8n91kcz04a0j3hs4s6
(spice/diode.osdi). Run: `nix develop .#testing -c tests/fixtures/families/f52/run.sh` (checked).
1. The DC law (dc_full_*, reltol 1e-9): equal to 1e-10 at 0.3 to 1.0 V. At the default reltol 1e-3 VACASK's operating
   point through rs is 4.3e-4 off the converged current where ngspice's is 5e-7 off (0.6 V: 0.1189911 against
   0.1190422): its convergence test accepts a looser solution.
2. The charge law (ac_*, tight tolerances): re and im of i(v1) at 1 kHz and 100 kHz, forward 0.3 to 0.7 V and reverse
   -0.5 V, with and without cjo: equal to 6e-11. Diffusion (tt·gd) and depletion capacitances are the same law.
3. The transient (tr_*): i(v1) at 0.4 us (before the recovery edge) and 0.5 us (after it).
   default tolerances: ngspice 1.115623 (stable from a 0.5 ns cap), VACASK 1.21375, 1.16662, 1.15695 at caps 0.5, 0.1,
   0.02 ns (diffs-2's F52 numbers).
   reltol 1e-6: VACASK 1.12909, 1.12198, 1.11791 (0.5, 0.1, 0.02 ns), 1.11610 at 4 ps; reltol 1e-8: 1.11659 (20 ps),
   1.11597 (4 ps); ngspice 1.115613 throughout. VACASK converges to ngspice's value, at first order in the step (its
   error falls 4.7x per fivefold refinement, 2.3e-3 to 4.9e-4 at reltol 1e-6), and its default tolerances leave 3.5 %.
Verdict: no law differs (DC and charge equal to 1e-10). The difference is VACASK's integration of this deck: first-order
convergence of the reverse recovery under its trapezoidal rule (the transit-time charge's implicit equation, F52) and a
default convergence test 1e3 times looser than ngspice's at the same reltol; refined, both reach 1.1156. Class:
convergence (R96 (3)), VACASK accused by its own refinement converging to ngspice's value.

